Hi Joe,

This is a complicated issue, so I understand your confusion! however, if you look at the model in more detail described on the page you are linking:

data {
  int<lower=0> N;
  int<lower=0, upper=1> y[N];
}
parameters {
  real alpha;
}
transformed parameters {
  real<lower=0, upper=1> theta;
  theta <- inv_logit(alpha);
}
model {
  for (n in 1:N)
    y[n] ~ bernoulli(theta);
  theta ~ uniform(0, 1);
}
, you will see that theta is the transformed variable on the left side in the transformed parameters block, but also the same variable being distributed across in the model block. In this case, it is necessary to use the Jacobian adjustment. In hBayesDM, we do the following (from ra_noLA):

transformed parameters {
  vector<lower=0,upper=2>[N] rho;
  vector<lower=0>[N] tau;

  for (i in 1:N) {
    rho[i] = Phi_approx( mu_p[1] + sigma[1] * rho_p[i] ) * 2;
  }
  tau = exp( mu_p[2] + sigma[2] * tau_p );
}
model {
  # ra_prospect: Original model in Soko-Hessner et al 2009 PNAS
  # hyper parameters
  mu_p  ~ normal(0, 1.0);
  sigma ~ cauchy(0, 5.0);

  # individual parameters w/ Matt trick
  rho_p ~ normal(0, 1.0);
  tau_p ~ normal(0, 1.0);
 ...

Here, you can see that we are distributing the normal along rho_p and tau_p, and then transforming these into rho and tau. In this case, the adjustment is not necessary.

I hope this helps!

Best,
Nate

